/* Apply coerces f x, a list of c and d, to q0 (lists of a and b), which
   fails, but only as the argument of H, which discards it.  The language is
   call-by-name: a coercion is checked when its value is demanded, so this
   one never is, and the output c (d e) is in q1: sat.  The reduction's
   coercion problem checks every coercion where it is written and refutes,
   so the answer is unknown, never unsat. */
%BEGING
Apply f x -> c (H (coerce q0 (f x))).
H y -> d e.
Copy x -> case x of
	 e => e
| a x' => a (Copy x')
| b x' => b (Copy x')
| c x' => c (Copy x')
| d x' => d (Copy x').
%ENDG

%BEGINA
q0 a -> q0.
q0 b -> q0.
q0 e -> .
q1 c -> q1.
q1 d -> q1.
q1 e -> .
%ENDA

%BEGINT
Apply : (q0 -> q1) -> q0 -> q1.
%ENDT
